<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Virginia Declaration of Rights</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> Declaration of Rights was adopted by the Virginia Convention on 12 June 1776, drafted mainly by George Mason. It declared that all men are by nature equally free and independent, with inherent rights to life, liberty and property, and protected freedom of the press and of religion. It influenced the Declaration of Independence, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-bill-of-rights">United States Bill of Rights</a> and the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Declaration was adopted unanimously by the Fifth Virginia Convention at Williamsburg on 12 June 1776, as a document separate from the Constitution of Virginia, which followed on 29 June 1776. George Mason drafted ten articles at about 20 to 26 May 1776, and three more were added in committee, in the handwriting of Thomas Ludwell Lee, though their author is unknown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> proposed liberalising the clause on religious freedom, and the convention made further changes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrick-henry">Patrick Henry</a> persuaded it to delete a ban on bills of attainder.</p><p>Mason based his draft on the English Bill of Rights of 1689 and the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>. Edmund Pendleton proposed the phrase when they enter into a state of society, which let slave holders support a declaration of universal rights that would be understood not to apply to slaves, who were not part of civil society. Mason owned over 100 slaves at the time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Declaration has sixteen articles on the rights that pertain to the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> as the basis and foundation of government. Article 1 holds that all men are by nature equally free and independent and have inherent rights, namely life and liberty, with the means of acquiring property, and pursuing happiness and safety. Articles 2 and 3 say that all power derives from the people and that a majority has a right to reform, alter or abolish an inadequate government. Article 4 rejects hereditary offices and exclusive privileges.</p><p>Articles 5 and 6 call for separate legislative, executive and judicial powers and for frequent, certain and regular elections. The later articles limit the government's powers and establish rights to confront accusers and to a speedy trial by an impartial jury, protection against self-incrimination, excessive bail and cruel and unusual punishments, and a ban on general warrants. Article 12 is the first codification of the right to a free press, and Article 16 holds that all men are equally entitled to the free exercise of religion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Declaration was incorporated into the Virginia Constitution in 1830 as Article I, and a slightly updated version remains in it. The Committee of Five is thought to have drawn on it for the Declaration of Independence in June 1776, and Madison was influenced by it when drafting the Bill of Rights, introduced in September 1789 and ratified in 1791.</p><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, the delegates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>'s western counties at the Wheeling Convention cited it against the state's Ordinance of Secession, arguing that a change in the form of government needed a referendum. This set in motion the events by which the western counties became the separate state of West Virginia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Virginia Declaration of Rights” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His most immediate sources were his own draft of the preamble of the Constitution of Virginia and George Mason's draft of the <b>Virginia Declaration of Rights</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Virginians such as Patrick Henry, George Washington and Thomas Jefferson led resistance to Britain, and in 1776 the colony adopted George Mason's <b>Declaration of Rights</b> and became an independent commonwealth within the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-virginia">Colony of Virginia</a></li></ul></section>
